### Setup and notation.
Let \( Q \in L^2(\mathbb{R}^d) \) be a nontrivial solution of the equation \( \Delta Q + \alpha |Q|^{p-1} Q = \beta Q \), under the standing assumption \( 1 < p < 1 + \frac{4}{d-2} \) (the lower bound on \( p \) being the natural range for a superlinear power nonlinearity). We work throughout with the natural energy space \( H^1(\mathbb{R}^d) \) (into which any \( L^2 \) solution is readily seen to bootstrap by standard elliptic regularity, given the subcriticality assumption on \( p \)). Write \( G := \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |\nabla Q|^2 \), \( M := \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |Q|^2 \), and \( N := \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |Q|^{p+1} \), so that \( G,\, M,\, N > 0 \).

### Integral identities.
Multiplying the equation through by \( Q \) and integrating by parts immediately yields the relation
\begin{equation} \label{eq:energy}
 -G + \alpha N = \beta M\,. \tag{1}
\end{equation}
Next, the Pohozaev identity associated to the equation \( -\Delta Q = \alpha |Q|^{p-1} Q - \beta Q \) (obtained in the usual way by multiplying through by \( x\cdot \nabla Q \) and integrating) reads \( \frac{d-2}{2} G = d \int F(Q) \), where \( F \) is a primitive of the nonlinearity, i.e., \( F(s) = \frac{\alpha}{p+1} |s|^{p+1} - \frac{\beta}{2} |s|^2 \). Expanding, this is
\begin{equation} \label{eq:poho}
 \frac{d-2}{2} G = \frac{d\alpha}{p+1} N - \frac{d\beta}{2} M\,. \tag{2}
\end{equation}

### Necessary conditions on the signs.
Substitute \( \beta M = \alpha N - G \) from \eqref{eq:energy} into \eqref{eq:poho} and rearrange, to obtain the relation \( -G = d\alpha \frac{1-p}{2(p+1)} N \), or equivalently
\begin{equation} \label{eq:G}
 G = d\alpha \frac{p-1}{2(p+1)} N\,. \tag{3}
\end{equation}
Since \( G,\, N > 0 \) and \( p > 1 \), we deduce immediately that necessarily \( \alpha > 0 \). (In particular this rules out the cases \( \alpha \leqslant 0 \).)

Next substitute \eqref{eq:G} back into \eqref{eq:energy}, to find \( \beta M = \alpha N \Bigl( 1 - \frac{d(p-1)}{2(p+1)} \Bigr) \). The prefactor \( \frac{\alpha N}{M} \) is positive, so the sign of \( \beta \) is the same as the sign of the term in parentheses. But the standing assumption \( p < 1 + \frac{4}{d-2} = \frac{d+2}{d-2} \) is precisely equivalent (for \( d \geqslant 3 \); with the cases \( d=1,2 \) being even more favourable) to the inequality \( 1 - \frac{d(p-1)}{2(p+1)} > 0 \). Thus necessarily \( \beta > 0 \).

### Sufficiency.
Conversely, if \( \alpha > 0 \) and \( \beta > 0 \), the equation may be rescaled to the standard form \( -\Delta v + v = |v|^{p-1} v \). (Explicitly, the rescaling \( Q(x) = \mu\, v(\lambda x) \) with \( \mu = \bigl( \frac{\beta}{\alpha} \bigr)^{\frac{1}{p-1}} \) and \( \lambda = \sqrt{\beta} \) reduces to this form.) Under the standing subcriticality assumption on \( p \), the latter equation is well-known to admit a nontrivial radial positive solution in \( H^1(\mathbb{R}^d) \) (e.g., by constrained minimisation of the Sobolev quotient, or by mountain-pass methods). Thus solutions exist precisely for \( \alpha > 0 \) and \( \beta > 0 \).

### Conclusion.
The equation admits a nontrivial \( L^2(\mathbb{R}^d) \) solution if and only if \( \alpha > 0 \) and \( \beta > 0 \).

FINAL ANSWER: B

Safety properties in distributed systems and blockchains (e.g., consistency of the ledger, absence of double-spends or conflicting finalized blocks) are finitely refutable: a violation, if it occurs, is observable after a finite prefix of the execution. Liveness properties (e.g., “every valid transaction is eventually included”) are not finitely refutable; any finite delay is still consistent with the property holding in the limit.

The observation that no transaction has been included for one day is a finite prefix. It therefore supplies no evidence that a safety violation has occurred, nor does it constitute a definitive violation of liveness. Consequently:

- A is false (safety is not known to be broken);
- B is false (liveness is not known to be broken);
- C is false (neither property is known to be broken);
- D is false (it is possible that both properties still hold).

The only remaining statement is therefore E.

FINAL ANSWER: E

A cartesian closed category has all finite products (in particular a terminal object) together with right adjoints to each functor \(-\times A\). An abelian category is in particular additive, hence possesses a zero object \(0\) that is simultaneously initial and terminal, and all finite products coincide with biproducts.

Consequently the terminal object of a cartesian closed abelian category \(\mathcal{C}\) is \(0\). The exponential adjunction therefore specialises, for every pair of objects \(A,B\), to a bijection of hom-sets
\[
\operatorname{Hom}(A,B)\;\simeq\;\operatorname{Hom}(0\times A,B)\;\simeq\;\operatorname{Hom}(0,B^A).
\]
The right-hand side is a singleton because \(0\) is initial. It follows that every hom-set of \(\mathcal{C}\) contains exactly one morphism. The unique endomorphism of any object is necessarily its identity, every object is a zero object, and \(\mathcal{C}\) is equivalent to the terminal category \(\mathbf{1}\) (one object, one morphism).

The terminal category is the Eilenberg–Moore category of algebras for the monad on \(\mathbf{Set}\) that sends every set to a singleton (unit the unique map to the singleton, multiplication the identity on the singleton). Hence a cartesian closed abelian category is the category of algebras of a monad.

All other listed claims fail for \(\mathbf{1}\):
- it is a topos but not two-valued;
- it possesses no non-identity morphism;
- it is trivial;
- it is equivalent neither to finite-dimensional vector spaces nor to any category of group representations;
- “rich structural properties” is too vague to be a precise statement;
- it is terminal, not initial, in \(\mathbf{Cat}\);
- its unique endomorphism is an identity.

Thus the only accurate assertion among the options is B.

FINAL ANSWER: B

The edge of chaos is the dynamical regime in which a complex adaptive system simultaneously maximises computational capacity, sensitivity to perturbations and long-term stability.  Consequently the governing equation must contain:

- a continuous-time, spatially extended evolution (\(\partial_t w=D\nabla^2w+\dots\)) that can support chaotic attractors and Lyapunov instability;
- multiplicative and additive noise terms (\(\beta(t)\varepsilon\), \(\xi\cdot m\)) that supply controlled stochasticity;
- activity-dependent homeostatic potentials (\(\int\theta\,d\tau\), \(\int\mathrm{Activity}\,d\tau\)) that automatically throttle updates and thereby prevent energetic or informational overload;
- an exponentially weighted memory integral that supplies the inertia needed for stability while still permitting adaptation;
- the usual gradient, regularisation and utility-driven learning terms that keep the network task-relevant.

Only the continuous PDE that retains every one of these contributions satisfies all of the above requirements at once.  That equation is option A.  The discrete maps (B,E) are merely Euler discretisations of the same PDE and therefore lose the natural continuum limit in which edge-of-chaos criticality is most cleanly expressed; the truncated continuous equations (C,D) omit the homeostatic and memory integrals and therefore cannot enforce the required energy/information bounds.

FINAL ANSWER: A

